Lab: Packet-Filtering & Proxy Firewall using iptables 

Objective
To configure a packet-filtering firewall using iptables, configure a proxy firewall using Squid, and compare their behavior in controlling network traffic.
Lab Overview
You will perform:
Configure iptables to allow/block traffic based on IP and ports.
Install Squid and configure it as an HTTP proxy firewall.
Compare how each protects the system.
Note: You may replace the IP with your actual IP using:
ip a
PART A: CONFIGURE PACKET-FILTERING FIREWALL USING iptables

View Current iptables Rules
Check kernel modules that support iptables:
sudo lsmod | egrep 'ip_tables|nf_conntrack|xt_' || true
This lists loaded kernel modules related to Netfilter (ip_tables, connection tracking modules, extensions). 
Type:
sudo iptables -L -v -n
sudo iptables -L -n -v --line-numbers
sudo iptables -S
This shows all existing rules and whether the firewall is active.
		sudo iptables -t filter -L -n --line-numbers
sudo iptables -t nat    -L -n --line-numbers
sudo iptables -t mangle -L -n --line-numbers
sudo iptables -t raw    -L -n --line-numbers
sudo iptables -t security -L -n --line-numbers || true

Note: When a packet arrives from the network, it first traverses raw PREROUTING, then mangle PREROUTING, then nat PREROUTING (for DNAT), then the routing decision is made. If the packet is destined for the local machine, it goes to the filter INPUT chain; if it is to be forwarded, it goes to the filter FORWARD chain. Locally generated packets go through raw OUTPUT, mangle OUTPUT, nat OUTPUT (if needed), then filter OUTPUT, and finally mangle POSTROUTING and nat POSTROUTING before leaving the host.

INPUT chain lab (packets destined to this host):

Explicit rules in the INPUT chain to allow SSH, allow HTTP, and drop everything else:

# flush previous rules to start clean (only do this on a test host)
sudo iptables -F

# default policy to DROP (safe: allow SSH)
sudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT
sudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j ACCEPT
sudo iptables -A INPUT -m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT
sudo iptables -P INPUT DROP
sudo iptables -P FORWARD DROP
sudo iptables -P OUTPUT ACCEPT

Test and observation:
From client, curl http://<server-ip> should succeed because we allowed port 80.
From client, telnet <server-ip> 22 should succeed (SSH) and other ports like 8080 should be blocked.

OUTPUT chain lab
Used for packets that are created by the local host and are going out to any external network interface.

Restrict outgoing connections by creating rules in the OUTPUT chain so that only DNS and HTTP requests are allowed from this host.

# reset
sudo iptables -F

# allow established replies
sudo iptables -A OUTPUT -m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT

# allow DNS (UDP 53 and TCP 53 for large responses/zone transfers)
sudo iptables -A OUTPUT -p udp --dport 53 -j ACCEPT
sudo iptables -A OUTPUT -p tcp --dport 53 -j ACCEPT

# allow HTTP and HTTPS outbound
sudo iptables -A OUTPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j ACCEPT
sudo iptables -A OUTPUT -p tcp --dport 443 -j ACCEPT

# drop all other outbound traffic
sudo iptables -P OUTPUT DROP
sudo iptables -P INPUT ACCEPT
sudo iptables -P FORWARD DROP

Test and observation:
On the host, run curl https://example.com — it should succeed.
Try ssh user@remote from the host — it should fail because outbound port 22 is not allowed.1
Revert:
sudo iptables -P OUTPUT ACCEPT
sudo iptables -F

FORWARD chain lab (routing/packet forwarding)

Enable IPv4 forwarding and set up a simple two-network forwarding test through this host.

# Enable IP forwarding temporarily
sudo sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=1

# Example: assume two NICs on the router/test host: eth0 (192.168.10.1) and eth1 (192.168.20.1)
# Clients: 192.168.10.100 and 192.168.20.100 on separate VMs or networks

# Clear rules
sudo iptables -F

# Allow forwarding for established connections
sudo iptables -A FORWARD -m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT

# Allow forwarding from net10 to net20
sudo iptables -A FORWARD -s 192.168.10.0/24 -d 192.168.20.0/24 -j ACCEPT

# Allow forwarding from net20 to net10 (optional)
sudo iptables -A FORWARD -s 192.168.20.0/24 -d 192.168.10.0/24 -j ACCEPT

# Default deny forwarding
sudo iptables -P FORWARD DROP
sudo iptables -P INPUT ACCEPT
sudo iptables -P OUTPUT ACCEPT

Test and observation:
From a host in 192.168.10.0/24, try to ping or ssh to a host in 192.168.20.0/24. It should succeed only if the FORWARD rules allow it.
If you remove the specific FORWARD accepts and leave default DROP, forwarding stops.
Revert:
sudo sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=0
sudo iptables -F
sudo iptables -P FORWARD ACCEPT

Processing order and a NAT example (PREROUTING/POSTROUTING)
Demonstrate how NAT works using the nat table PREROUTING and POSTROUTING chains by performing source NAT (MASQUERADE).
# Example: we have internal network on eth1 (192.168.50.0/24) and internet on eth0
sudo iptables -t nat -A POSTROUTING -o eth0 -s 192.168.50.0/24 -j MASQUERADE

# Allow forwarding as needed
sudo sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=1
sudo iptables -A FORWARD -s 192.168.50.0/24 -o eth0 -j ACCEPT
sudo iptables -A FORWARD -d 192.168.50.0/24 -m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT
Note: The nat POSTROUTING MASQUERADE rule rewrites source addresses of outgoing packets so return traffic will come back to the NAT gateway and be forwarded to the correct internal host.
Test and observation:
From an internal host, browse the internet — external servers will see requests from the gateway’s external IP address.
Revert:
		sudo iptables -t nat -F
sudo iptables -F
sudo sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=0


