How to View Geoblocking Logs?

You need to insert a rule that uses the LOG target to write an entry to the kernel log (dmesg or /var/log/syslog) before the packet is dropped.

You will need to re-insert the GeoIP rules, putting a LOG rule immediately before each DROP rule. Since we are using -I (Insert at the top), we need to insert the DROP rule first, and then the LOG rule right before it.

First, clean up the existing GeoIP rules:

sudo iptables -D OUTPUT 1
sudo iptables -D INPUT 1
sudo ip6tables -D OUTPUT 1
sudo ip6tables -D INPUT 1

Next, add the LOG and DROP rules:

sudo iptables -I OUTPUT 1 -m geoip --dst-cc CN -j LOG --log-prefix "GEO_DROP_OUT: "
sudo iptables -I OUTPUT 2 -m geoip --dst-cc CN -j DROP

sudo ip6tables -I OUTPUT 1 -m geoip --dst-cc CN -j LOG --log-prefix "GEO_DROP_OUT6: "
sudo ip6tables -I OUTPUT 2 -m geoip --dst-cc CN -j DROP

After you add the logging rules, try to ping 180.76.76.76 again. The successful block will now generate a log entry you can view in the kernel ring buffer.
You can view the new log entries using the dmesg command and filtering for your custom prefix:
dmesg | grep "GEO_DROP_OUT"


