Internet Sharing Using iptables

Enable Packet Forwarding
Packet forwarding allows Kali to act as a router:
	echo 1 | sudo tee /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_forward

Make it permanent:
	echo "net.ipv4.ip_forward=1" | sudo tee -a /etc/sysctl.conf

Configure NAT (Masquerading)
NAT hides client IP addresses behind Kali.
Assume your internet-facing interface is eth0:
	sudo iptables -t nat -A POSTROUTING -o eth0 -j MASQUERADE
This ensures the client’s traffic is rewritten correctly and gets internet access.

Allow Forwarding Through the Firewall
You must permit NEW and ESTABLISHED client connections:
		sudo iptables -A FORWARD -m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT
sudo iptables -A FORWARD -m conntrack --ctstate NEW -j ACCEPT
This converts your firewall into a working router with SPI (stateful) behavior.

Set the Client to Use Kali as Default Gateway
Windows: Set via IPv4 properties
Linux:
sudo ip route replace default via 192.168.x.y
Now all client traffic goes through Kali.

