Iptables use case: Fail2ban 
	
Fail2ban is a security tool for Linux that automatically bans IP addresses when they fail to authenticate too many times. If someone tries to brute-force your SSH, FTP, web login, etc., Fail2ban will detect it and block their IP using iptables. It protects your system by watching log files (like /var/log/auth.log) and adding temporary iptables DROP rules for attackers.

Fail2ban workflow (very simple):
Someone tries to login to SSH with wrong password
Fail2ban sees multiple failures in /var/log/auth.log
After (example) 3 failures, Fail2ban: adds an iptables rule to block that IP and bans the IP for a certain time (example ban = 10 minutes).
The attacker cannot try again
After ban time ends, Fail2ban removes the rule automatically
Thus Fail2ban used iptables and provides automation on top of it.

We will use the same IP forwarding setup (refer to previous experiment) for this lab also.

Note: When firewall DNAT forwards port 2222 to the internal server’s port 22, the internal server still sees the client source IP, so Fail2ban will ban the real offender.

On Internal Kali#2 (192.168.1.13) — install & configure Fail2ban:

Create/modify local jail config (never edit /etc/fail2ban/jail.conf directly; use jail.local or /etc/fail2ban/jail.d/)
		sudo tee /etc/fail2ban/jail.d/sshd.local > /dev/null <<'EOF'
[sshd]
enabled = true
port    = ssh
filter  = sshd
logpath = /var/log/auth.log
maxretry = 3
findtime = 600
bantime = 300
action = iptables-multiport[name=SSH, port="ssh", protocol=tcp]
EOF

Restart/enable fail2ban
		sudo systemctl restart fail2ban
sudo systemctl enable fail2ban

Expected output from status sshd will show Enabled: true and Banned IP list : (initially empty).

Ensure SSH logs are generated — try a successful SSH login locally to see log activity:

	# from 192.168.1.13 itself:
ssh localhost
# then exit, and check logs
sudo tail -n 20 /var/log/auth.log

You should see sshd login entries. Fail2ban monitors this file.

